<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Percy family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as House of Percy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Percy family were a powerful English noble house in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a>, Earls of Northumberland from 1377. Henry Percy, Earl of Northumberland, and his son Harry Hotspur helped Henry IV to the throne, then rebelled against him, joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a> and Edmund Mortimer; Hotspur was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shrewsbury">Shrewsbury</a> in 1403. The family remained leading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> of the north for centuries.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/percy-family.jpg" alt="Percy family" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wikimandia · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Modern_arms_of_Percy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The house descends from William de Percy (d. 1096), a Norman who crossed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> in December 1067, took his name from the manor of Percy-en-Auge in Normandy and became the first feudal baron of Topcliffe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a>. By the reign of Henry II the line was represented only by an heiress, Agnes de Percy. Adeliza of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Louvain</a>, widow of Henry I, married her to her own young half-brother Joscelin of Louvain, a nobleman from Brabant, whose descendants took the name Percy.</p><p>In 1309 Henry de Percy, 1st Baron Percy, bought Alnwick Castle from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antony-bek">Antony Bek</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of Durham, and from then on the family's fortunes were tied to Alnwick. The 2nd Baron received Warkworth in 1328 and commanded the right wing of the English army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nevilles-cross">Battle of Neville's Cross</a> in 1346. As nearly every Percy was a Warden of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marche">the Marches</a>, Scottish affairs often concerned them more than English ones.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the 1st Earl's rebellion his estates were forfeited, but Henry V restored Hotspur's son as 2nd Earl. The long Percy-Neville feud with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neville-family">House of Neville</a> fed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>, in which the Percys were staunch Lancastrians and the 3rd Earl and three of his brothers lost their lives. The 4th Earl's slowness at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bosworth-field">Battle of Bosworth Field</a>, through indecision or treachery, helped bring down <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-iii-of-england">Richard III</a>, and in 1489 his own tenants pulled him from his horse and murdered him.</p><p>The 6th Earl was the accepted suitor of Anne Boleyn before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> married her. Later the Rising of the North, the plots to free <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-queen-of-scots">Mary, Queen of Scots</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gunpowder-plot">Gunpowder Plot</a> each drew in a Percy, and the 8th and 9th Earls spent years in the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, while the 10th Earl, Algernon, fought against King Charles in the Civil War. The male line ended with Joceline Percy, 11th Earl, who died in 1670.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>His daughter Elizabeth married Charles Seymour, 6th Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>. Their son Algernon Seymour was created Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a> in 1749, with the title to pass to Sir Hugh Smithson, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yorkshire">Yorkshire</a> baronet who had married Algernon's daughter Elizabeth. Smithson took the name Percy and in 1766 was made the first Duke of Northumberland. His descendants hold the dukedom, with Alnwick Castle as their seat, and the dukes are buried in the Northumberland Vault of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>, the last family to keep that privilege.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Percy family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He allied with France and with English rebels, the <b>Percys</b> and Mortimers, and in the Pennal Letter proposed an independent Welsh church and two universities.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-wales-under-owain-glyndwr">Principality of Wales under Owain Glyndwr</a></li></ul></section>
